<Page {...meta}>
  <h1>What to Ask AI About Your Training Runs</h1>
  <div class="postmeta">October 13, 2026 · Training</div>

  <article>
    <p>Your watch records everything and tells you almost nothing about what
    to do next. An AI assistant is good at the next step: reading a pile of
    splits and telling you what stands out. The quality of the answer depends
    almost entirely on what you give it and what you ask. Here is how to do
    both well.</p>

    <h2>What to share</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Splits, not screenshots.</strong> Mile or kilometer splits as
      plain text. Copy them from your app or export a CSV.</li>
      <li><strong>Context for each run.</strong> Was it an easy run, a tempo, a
      long run, a race? Heat, hills, and how you felt matter as much as the
      numbers.</li>
      <li><strong>The goal.</strong> The race, the date, and the pace you
      want. Without it, advice has nothing to aim at.</li>
      <li><strong>A few weeks, not one run.</strong> One run is noise. A
      pattern across four to eight weeks is information.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Five questions worth asking</h2>
    <h3>1. Did I run an even effort?</h3>
    <blockquote class="prompt">
      <p>Here are the mile splits from my 16 mile long run. Did I go out too
      fast? Where did I start to slow down, and by how much? Was this a
      positive split, negative split, or even?</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Fading late in long runs is the most useful thing to catch, because it
    is exactly what happens in the marathon. If your long runs fade, your
    race plan should expect it.</p>

    <h3>2. Is my easy pace actually easy?</h3>
    <blockquote class="prompt">
      <p>These are my last ten easy runs. Compare my easy pace to my tempo and
      race pace. Am I running easy days too hard to recover for the hard
      ones?</p>
    </blockquote>

    <h3>3. Is my goal pace supported by my training?</h3>
    <blockquote class="prompt">
      <p>My goal marathon pace is 8:35 per mile. Based on these runs, what pace
      have I actually shown I can hold, and for how long? What is the gap?</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>This is the honest question. Put the same numbers into the calculator's
    equivalency tool and compare. If the AI and the calculator land in the same
    neighborhood, believe both. If they disagree, find out why before race
    day.</p>

    <h3>4. What is the weakest part of my training?</h3>
    <blockquote class="prompt">
      <p>Based on my weekly mileage and longest runs over the last 8 weeks,
      what is the biggest gap between my training and a marathon? What is the
      one thing I should change?</p>
    </blockquote>

    <h3>5. How should my race plan reflect all this?</h3>
    <blockquote class="prompt">
      <p>Given what you see, suggest a segment based pace plan for the marathon:
      how fast to start, where to hold, and what to do if the last 10K falls
      apart.</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Then build whatever it suggests as segments in the calculator. Seeing
    the splits and the finish time turns advice into a plan you can run.</p>

    <h2>Check it before you trust it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Recompute any pace or time it gives you. Fluent does not mean
      correct.</li>
      <li>Ask it what it is assuming and what data it would need to be more
      sure.</li>
      <li>Compare its read of your fade to what you remember feeling. If they
      disagree, your memory of the run is data too.</li>
      <li>Treat pain, dizziness, or anything medical as a reason to see a
      professional, not an assistant.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>AI will not run the miles, and it does not know your body better than
    you do. What it does well is notice patterns you were too close to see.
    Pair that with a calculator that shows the plan, and you walk to the start
    line knowing why you picked your pace.</p>
  </article>

